{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat test/yin/repl/fixture.cljc docs/agents/build-n-test.md'","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.repl.fixture\n  \"Step 1 non-DHT composition fixture (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md\n   sections 8-10, Gate B): the common test constructor the retained engine\n   suite is parameterized over.  It injects an explicit `dao.jing` byte\n   handle into `yin.repl.frontends/create-state` -- never a store spec that\n   could resolve to DHT -- in two backend selections:\n\n   - `:memory` -- `dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem`, ephemeral.  Local\n     publication completion is acknowledged without claiming persistence\n     (spec section 4: `:durability :memory`).\n   - `:file` -- `dao.space.store/open` over a scratch directory, which\n     wraps `dao.jing.file/create-content-file` and supplies locking, the\n     atomic HEAD write (`:head-fn`), and validated recovery.  Local\n     publication is durable only when that HEAD write succeeds\n     (`:durability :persistent`).\n\n   The fixture owns the publication interpreter the spec assigns to\n   composition: `observed-backend` / `failable-backend` wrap the handle's\n   `:head-fn` -- the durable-publication request the engine emits today\n   through `yin.repl.index`'s `:after-publish`, after content materialized\n   and read back -- and record each call as an explicit section 4 result\n   record (`{:op :publication-result :id :round :manifest :status\n   :durability}`; `:status` is `:published` only when the HEAD write\n   returned, `:refused` with `:reason` when it threw).  Today's seam\n   carries only the manifest, so the fixture mints `:id` / `:round` as\n   the call sequence in emission order; Step 2's request record\n   (`{:op :publish :id :round :manifest}`) will carry the engine's own\n   identifiers and the fixture will check them instead of minting them.\n   The frontier expectations pinned through this fixture are stated so\n   they survive that move unchanged.\n\n   This namespace requires no DHT namespace (`dao.jing.dht`,\n   `dao.space.dht`, `yin.repl.dht`, `yin.vm.linker.dht`): engine tests\n   built on it construct no DHT node, socket, follower, or repair\n   scheduler.  (`yin.repl` itself still loads the DHT composition today;\n   removing that load is Step 2, gated by Gate A.)\"\n  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:io\" :as dart-io]])\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\n            [dao.space.index :as index]\n            [dao.space.store :as durable]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yin.repl.frontends :as repl.frontends]\n            [yin.repl.index :as repl.index]))\n\n\n(def backend-kinds\n  \"The non-DHT backend selections every parameterized engine test runs.\"\n  [:memory :file])\n\n\n(defn backend-durability\n  \"The local durability a successful publication claims on `kind`\n   (spec section 4): the memory composition never claims persistence.\"\n  [kind]\n  (case kind\n    :memory :memory\n    :file :persistent))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Publication-result protocol (spec section 4): the Step 2 oracle\n;;\n;; The engine prepares a frontier, emits `{:op :publish :id :round\n;; :manifest}`, and promotes that frontier only for the matching local\n;; result `{:op :publication-result :id :round :manifest :status\n;; :durability}`.  Identifiers are unique within the session and survive\n;; evaluation rollback; stream append success means accepted, never\n;; completed; network acknowledgement is a distinct outcome and never\n;; promotes.  `publication-result-expectations` states every case's\n;; required frontier effect; `accept-result` is the test-local model of\n;; `yin.repl/accept-publication-result`'s frontier rule that Step 2 must\n;; satisfy through the real stream seam without changing these outcomes.\n;; Cases marked `:step-2-oracle` cannot be delivered through today's\n;; synchronous `:head-fn` seam at all; the synchronous pins exercise only\n;; `:synchronous` cases.\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn publication-request\n  \"The section 4 publication request for one prepared frontier.\"\n  [id round manifest]\n  {:op :publish :id id :round round :manifest manifest})\n\n\n(defn publication-result\n  \"The section 4 local publication result answering `request`.\n   `status` is `:published` or `:refused`; a refusal carries `:reason`.\n   `durability` is the composition's local claim only (`:memory` or\n   `:persistent`), never a network acknowledgement.\"\n  [request status durability & [reason]]\n  (cond-> {:op :publication-result\n           :id (:id request)\n           :round (:round request)\n           :manifest (:manifest request)\n           :status status\n           :durability durability}\n    reason (assoc :reason reason)))\n\n\n(def publication-result-expectations\n  \"Expected frontier outcome for every publication-result case, stated\n   against a prior promoted frontier F, a prepared pending request\n   P = {:id i :round r :manifest m} covering round r, and a result R.\n   `:frontier` `:promote` means F becomes exactly P's frontier (manifest,\n   published count, and verified cursor together, exactly once);\n   `:preserve` means F is identical afterwards; `:ignore` means F is\n   identical and P stays pending.  `:pinned` separates what today's\n   synchronous seam can deliver from the Step 2 stream oracle.\"\n  [{:case :matching-success\n    :given \"R matches P (id, round, manifest) with :status :published\"\n    :frontier :promote :pending-after :cleared :pinned :synchronous}\n   {:case :refusal\n    :given \"R matches P with :status :refused and a :reason\"\n    :frontier :preserve :pending-after :superseded :pinned :synchronous\n    :note \"P's prepared manifest is never promoted; the file HEAD still names F; a later round's success promotes the whole suffix.\"}\n   {:case :delayed-completion\n    :given \"R matches P with :status :published, delivered after idle steps or after newer transactions committed but before any newer promotion\"\n    :frontier :promote :pending-after :cleared :pinned :step-2-oracle\n    :note \"No promotion and no settled report before R arrives; on arrival exactly P promotes. If a newer round already promoted, R is stale instead.\"}\n   {:case :wrong-id\n    :given \"R's :id differs from P's (round and manifest may match)\"\n    :frontier :ignore :pending-after :intact :pinned :step-2-oracle}\n   {:case :wrong-round\n    :given \"R's :round differs from P's (id and manifest may match)\"\n    :frontier :ignore :pending-after :intact :pinned :step-2-oracle}\n   {:case :wrong-manifest\n    :given \"R's :manifest differs from P's (id and round match)\"\n    :frontier :ignore :pending-after :intact :pinned :step-2-oracle\n    :note \"Neither R's manifest nor P's frontier promotes.\"}\n   {:case :duplicate\n    :given \"The matching :published R is delivered a second time after it promoted\"\n    :frontier :ignore :pending-after :intact :pinned :step-2-oracle\n    :note \"Frontier, counts, and cursor are identical; no second promotion and no new request. Re-stepping an idle indexer is not this case.\"}\n   {:case :stale\n    :given \"R names an already-promoted or superseded round (older than the published frontier)\"\n    :frontier :ignore :pending-after :intact :pinned :step-2-oracle\n    :note \"A stale result cannot promote another round and cannot regress the frontier, even across rollback.\"}])\n\n\n(defn accept-result\n  \"Test-local model of the section 4 frontier rule: `[frontier pending\n   result]`, where `pending` is `{:request req :promotes frontier'}` or\n   nil.  Answers `{:frontier f :pending p :effect e}` with `e` in\n   `:promoted` / `:preserved` / `:ignored`.  Only an exact id/round/\n   manifest match settles P; a mismatch leaves P intact, a refusal\n   supersedes P without touching the frontier, and a result with no\n   matching pending (duplicate or stale) changes nothing.\"\n  [frontier pending result]\n  (let [request (:request pending)]\n    (cond\n      (nil? pending)\n      {:frontier frontier :pending nil :effect :ignored}\n\n      (not (and (= (:id result) (:id request))\n                (= (:round result) (:round request))\n                (= (:manifest result) (:manifest request))))\n      {:frontier frontier :pending pending :effect :ignored}\n\n      (= :refused (:status result))\n      {:frontier frontier :pending nil :effect :preserved}\n\n      (= :published (:status result))\n      {:frontier (:promotes pending) :pending nil :effect :promoted}\n\n      :else\n      {:frontier frontier :pending pending :effect :ignored})))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Scratch directories (host-portable, as in yin.repl.store-test)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn temp-dir\n  \"A fresh scratch directory path for a `:file` backend.\"\n  []\n  (str \"target/test-fixture-\" (random-uuid)))\n\n\n(defn cleanup-dir!\n  \"Best-effort recursive removal of a scratch directory.\"\n  [dir]\n  (when dir\n    #?(:cljd (try (.deleteSync (dart-io/Directory. dir) .recursive true)\n                  (catch Object _ nil))\n       :clj (let [f (java.io.File. ^String dir)]\n              (when (.isDirectory f)\n                (doseq [child (.listFiles f)]\n                  (.delete ^java.io.File child))\n                (.delete f)))\n       :cljs (try (.rmSync (js/require \"fs\") dir #js {:recursive true :force true})\n                  (catch :default _ nil)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Backends: open / close / call-with\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn open-backend\n  \"Open the non-DHT backend `kind` (`:memory` or `:file`) as a backend\n   value `{:kind :handle :dir :durability}`.  `opts` may carry\n   `:publish-opts` (the immutable generic publication policy, spec\n   section 5 -- threaded onto the handle where `yin.repl/make-session`\n   reads it) and, for `:file`, an explicit `:dir`.\n\n   The handle is a plain `dao.jing` byte handle in both selections; the\n   file selection additionally carries `:head-fn` and `:recovery` from\n   `dao.space.store/open`.  No selection constructs DHT state.\"\n  ([kind] (open-backend kind {}))\n  ([kind {:keys [publish-opts dir]}]\n   (case kind\n     :memory (let [handle (cond-> (mem/create-content-mem)\n                            publish-opts (assoc :publish-opts publish-opts))]\n               {:kind :memory :handle handle :dir nil\n                :durability :memory})\n     :file (let [dir (or dir (temp-dir))\n                 opened (durable/open dir)\n                 handle (cond-> opened\n                          publish-opts (assoc :publish-opts publish-opts))]\n             {:kind :file :handle handle :dir dir\n              :durability :persistent}))))\n\n\n(defn close-backend!\n  \"Close a backend's handle (releasing a file backend's directory lock)\n   and remove its scratch directory.  Idempotent in effect: a second\n   close of a content handle is refused by nothing here.\"\n  [{:keys [handle dir]}]\n  (when-let [close! (:close-fn handle)]\n    (try (close!) (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) _ nil)))\n  (cleanup-dir! dir)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn call-with-backend\n  \"Open backend `kind`, call `(f backend)`, and close the backend even\n   when `f` throws.  Answers `f`'s value; anything a test needs from the\n   store must be read inside `f`, before the close.\"\n  ([kind f] (call-with-backend kind {} f))\n  ([kind opts f]\n   (let [backend (open-backend kind opts)]\n     (try (f backend)\n          (finally (close-backend! backend))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The common test constructor (Gate B's injection point)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn create-state\n  \"A shell over `backend`'s explicitly injected `dao.jing` handle, plus\n   `opts` for `yin.repl.frontends/create-state` (`:vm-type`,\n   `:content-store`, `:name-env`, ...).  This is Gate B's constructor:\n   the proof never relies on the default memory construction, and no\n   `:index-store-spec` -- the only path that can name DHT -- is used.\"\n  ([backend] (create-state backend {}))\n  ([backend opts]\n   (repl.frontends/create-state (merge {:index-store (:handle backend)} opts))))\n\n\n(defn call-with-state\n  \"Open backend `kind`, build a shell over it with `opts`, call\n   `(f state backend)`, and close the backend afterwards.\"\n  ([kind f] (call-with-state kind {} f))\n  ([kind opts f]\n   (call-with-backend kind (fn [backend] (f (create-state backend opts) backend)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Publication observation: the composition-side interpreter (Step 1 form)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- recording-head-fn\n  \"The Step 1 composition interpreter over one backend's `:head-fn`:\n   each call mints the next section 4 correlation (`:id` and `:round`\n   are the call sequence, because today's seam carries only the\n   manifest) and appends exactly one result record to `log` -- after\n   the HEAD write's outcome is known, so a refusal is never recorded\n   as a success.  While `refuse?` holds true the write is refused\n   without delegating.  The write's exception always propagates.\"\n  [log sequence inner durability refuse?]\n  (fn [manifest-address]\n    (let [n (swap! sequence inc)\n          request (publication-request n n manifest-address)]\n      (if (and refuse? @refuse?)\n        (do (swap! log conj (publication-result request :refused\n                                                durability \"HEAD refused\"))\n            (throw (ex-info \"HEAD refused\" {})))\n        (try\n          (when inner (inner manifest-address))\n          (swap! log conj (publication-result request :published durability))\n          (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n            (swap! log conj (publication-result\n                              request :refused durability\n                              (or (ex-message e) \"HEAD refused\")))\n            (throw e)))))))\n\n\n(defn publication-requests\n  \"The section 4 requests a result `log` answers, oldest first: each\n   result's `:id` / `:round` / `:manifest` projected back to its\n   `{:op :publish ...}` request.\"\n  [log-values]\n  (mapv #(publication-request (:id %) (:round %) (:manifest %)) log-values))\n\n\n(defn observed-backend\n  \"Backend whose durable-publication results are recorded in `log` (an\n   atom of vectors): each `:head-fn` call appends one\n   `{:op :publication-result :id :round :manifest :status :durability}`\n   record (`:refused` results also carry `:reason`).  A memory backend\n   has no `:head-fn` today; observing one installs a recording writer\n   that acknowledges local completion in memory only -- it never makes\n   the ephemeral store claim persistence.\"\n  [backend log]\n  (let [handle (:handle backend)]\n    (assoc backend :handle\n           (assoc handle :head-fn\n                  (recording-head-fn log (atom 0) (:head-fn handle)\n                                     (:durability backend) nil)))))\n\n\n(defn failable-backend\n  \"Like `observed-backend`, but while `fail?` (an atom) holds true the\n   HEAD write is refused instead of delegating: the log gains a\n   `:refused` result and the caller sees the refusal throw.  Tests read\n   attempts and outcomes from the same log: `:status` distinguishes\n   them, and only `:published` manifests may appear as the promoted\n   frontier.\"\n  [backend log fail?]\n  (let [handle (:handle backend)]\n    (assoc backend :handle\n           (assoc handle :head-fn\n                  (recording-head-fn log (atom 0) (:head-fn handle)\n                                     (:durability backend) fail?)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Frontier reading (spec section 8, Frontier gate)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn stream-values\n  \"Every value currently on stream `s`, read from its oldest cursor.\"\n  [s]\n  (loop [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor s :dao.stream/oldest))\n         acc []]\n    (let [r (stream/next s cursor)]\n      (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n        (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r) (conj acc (:dao.stream/value r)))\n        acc))))\n\n\n(defn transactions\n  \"The transaction records an indexer committed, oldest first.\"\n  [indexer]\n  (mapv :dao.space/transaction (stream-values (:local indexer))))\n\n\n(defn expected-branching-factor\n  \"The count cap `indexer` publishes under: its declared publication\n   policy's `:branching-factor`, defaulting to 512 as the engine does.\n   A manifest is count-cap compatible only when its own\n   `:branching-factor` equals this (spec sections 5 and 8).\"\n  [indexer]\n  (:branching-factor (:publish-opts indexer) 512))\n\n\n(defn frontier\n  \"The indexer's verified published frontier as plain data, with the\n   manifest read back from its content store: everything the Frontier\n   gate (spec section 8) requires to agree -- manifest identity,\n   published transaction count, count-cap compatibility, cursor\n   ownership, and coverage.  `:manifest` is nil before any publication.\"\n  [indexer]\n  (let [address (:manifest-address indexer)\n        manifest (when address\n                   (index/read-manifest (:content-store indexer) address))]\n    {:manifest-address address\n     :manifest manifest\n     :manifest-identity (when manifest (jing/segment-key manifest))\n     :count (:count manifest)\n     :branching-factor (:branching-factor manifest)\n     :expected-branching-factor (expected-branching-factor indexer)\n     :published (:published indexer)\n     :transactions (:transactions indexer)\n     :materialization (:materialization indexer)\n     :materialized-transactions (get-in indexer [:materialization :transactions])\n     :cursor (get-in indexer [:materialization :cursor])\n     :newest-cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\n                      (stream/cursor (:local indexer) :dao.stream/newest))\n     :status (repl.index/status indexer)}))\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n\n```sh\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n```\n\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\n\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\n\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\n\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\n\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\n\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\n\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\n\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\n\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\n\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\n\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>`. Fix against the failing test alone,\n   re-run it green, then climb back up (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \"Cadence\" below), so a\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\n\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\n\"nightly.clj\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\nmistake, not a host outage.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\n\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\ntree that did not contain them.\n\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\n\n```sh\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\ngit fetch -q origin \"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\"\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\n```\n\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \"$URL\"`). If\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\n\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\n\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\nfirst lane:\n\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\n   only (\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\n\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\n\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\n\n```sh\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\n```\n\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\nresource fails there immediately.\n\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\n\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\nchange to a ledger file:\n\n```sh\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\n```\n\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\nstaging.\n\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\n\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\n5-6-hour run.\n\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\n\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\n\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \"a second UDP bind on\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \"127.0.0.1\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\n\nOwner, verbatim: \"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\nchanges\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \"it should not be for\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\"\n\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\n\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\n  documented under \"Cadence\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\n  Its results are a\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\n\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\nnightly owns the follow-up.\n\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\npre-existing by the owning seat.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\n\"free laptop\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\n\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\n\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\n**Dart quick tier\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\n\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\n\"Patch does not apply\" or \"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\".\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\n\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\n\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \"run tests remotely\nover SSH\" to \"delegate whole work agents remotely\": a heavy implementation or\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\n\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\nreference):\n\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\n   prepare the scratch clone per \"Get the code there\".\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \"$(mise env -s\n   bash)\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\n   inside the same command.\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\n\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md'","aggregated_output":"# yin.repl DHT composition: the engine depends only on dao.jing\n\nStatus: governing spec for the DHT-boundary refactor. This document\nsynthesizes the architect charter\n(`collab/dao-stream/1794062607000-architect-refactor-first-ruling-gpt6astra.findings.md`)\nand its supplement\n(`collab/dao-stream/1794109245000-architect-charter-supplement-gaps.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`).\nIt invents no new design: every rule below is drawn from those two\nartifacts and from the owner requirements recorded in section 1.\nImplementation follows this spec; where code and this spec disagree, the\nspec governs until an architect round amends it.\n\n## 1. Owner requirements and scope\n\nThe owner requirements, stated in conversation and recorded verbatim\nwhere quoted, bound everything that follows:\n\n> \"yin.repl depends only on dao.jing. It should not know anything about\n> dao.jing.dht so that any implementation of dao.jing can be swapped out\"\n\n> \"dao.stream is framed as a stream of bytes\"\n\nThe second ruling has a consequence this spec carries through: `dao.stream`\nstays a byte stream; chunking and framing are transport-layer concerns\nbelow the logical stream interface. The earlier chunk-contract addendum\nthat promoted transport chunks and acknowledgements into the stream\ncontract was superseded by this ruling and does not survive.\n\nTwo further owner rulings from the same conversation apply throughout.\nFrame budgets derive from the transport composition and are never\nhardcoded by consumers. `dao.space` implements semantic page cuts. Neither\nruling is a local guideline: frame budgets, page cuts, and the stream\ncontract are treated below as invariants, not preferences.\n\nThe charter's disposition is refactor-first for the DHT boundary.\nIndependently verified fixes may merge separately. R7 — byte-cutting the\nexisting coupled REPL composition on `nightly-reds-fix` before extraction\n— is overturned. R1–R3 remain storage work within the refactor sequence,\nsubject to the corrections in section 12. The properties that passed\narchitectural review are transport ignorance in the engine, explicit\ncomposition ownership, transport-derived framing limits, semantic page\ncutting in storage, unchanged manifest schema and count-cap meaning,\nindependent evaluator and index observers, and separation of local\ndurability from network acknowledgement. Runtime preservation and\nreadiness to merge remain unverified until the gates in section 10 pass.\n\nThis spec governs one refactor: separating the engine in `yin.repl` from\nevery DHT concern so that the engine's storage dependency is `dao.jing`\nalone. The relocated module is a small public composition surface\noutside `yin.repl` — illustratively `open`, `step`, and `close!` — that\nowns the engine value alongside transport state. The engine does not own\nor step that transport state. For naming clarity the supplement uses\n`yin.shell.*` as the proposed composition namespace family, outside\n`yin.repl`. Those are target names, not existing APIs.\n\n## 2. The dependency rule\n\nThe engine depends only on `dao.jing` for its storage implementation.\nThat sentence constrains the storage-implementation dependency. It does\nnot remove the engine's legitimate `dao.space` indexing and query\ndependencies or its `dao.stream` dependencies, which remain. What it\nforbids is any knowledge of a particular storage backend inside the\nengine tree: no reference to `dao.jing.dht`, and no indirect reference\nthat reaches the same implementation through another namespace.\n\nThe rule is stated as two invariants, enforced by the gates in\nsection 10. Gate A requires zero storage-implementation references in the\nengine tree. Gate B requires the retained engine suite to pass with\nexplicitly injected non-DHT storage. Both gates must hold for the\nboundary merge; neither alone is sufficient. A namespace move that\nrenames `:dht` to an opaque runner while the engine continues to\nmanipulate that runner's state does not satisfy the rule. Renaming is\nnot extraction.\n\nThe current coupling violates the rule at construction and beyond it.\nEngine construction selects and opens the DHT composition and then\nretains its node (`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:942`). Linker progression,\nquery services, rollback state, and event filtering also carry DHT\nknowledge (`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1355`,\n`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:66`). Extracting only the module named in\nthe dispute therefore fails the boundary gate: the adapters in\nsection 7 must move with the module.\n\nA necessary dependency follow-through applies to the linker. Moving the\ncalls listed in section 6 is insufficient if the engine still imports\n`yin.vm.linker.interpreter`, which itself imports `dao.space.dht` and\n`yin.vm.linker.dht` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc:35`). The\nrefactor must split that interpreter's generic pair, retirement, and\nadmission machinery from DHT serving, or move ownership of the\ninterpreter entirely to composition. Its current `round [linker node]`\ninterface cannot remain an engine dependency.\n\n## 3. Ownership split and ordering contract\n\nThe amended boundary is this: the engine receives a `dao.jing` content\nhandle and explicit service media; composition owns storage selection,\ndurable publication, and every DHT concern. The split is exhaustive.\nAnything not listed under engine ownership below belongs to composition.\n\n| Boundary | Minimum contract |\n|---|---|\n| Engine construction | A `dao.jing` content-store handle, an immutable generic publication policy, validated recovery data, and explicit request/result streams. No peer, cookie, repair-slot, or DHT-node options. |\n| Engine publication output | A correlated publication request containing the round and an opaque manifest address, emitted after the necessary content has materialized and read back successfully. |\n| Composition publication result | Explicit local durable-publication success or failure. Network dissemination results remain distinct from local durability. |\n| Recovery and link input | Validated recovery installation and generic content/link completion data. The engine does not decode DHT event keywords. |\n| Composition ownership | Store opening, HEAD persistence, announce, signed-head deposit and following, hydration, transport stepping, scheduling, banners, refusal rendering, and shutdown. |\n\nComposition additionally owns store opening and locking, recovery\nvalidation, atomic durable HEAD replacement, signed-head deposit and\nfollowing, announce, hydration, repair, transport stepping, rendering,\nand shutdown. The engine owns evaluation, indexing over the injected\nhandle, preparation of publication requests, and consumption of generic\npublication results, recovery installations, and link and query\ncompletions. The linker, query, rollback, and event-filtering adapters\nare composition-side interpreters of explicit streams; they are not\ninjected callbacks and not an opaque runner whose internal state the\nengine still manipulates (section 7).\n\nThe ordering contract is preserved exactly. Content is materialized and\nread back, then the durable HEAD write succeeds, and only then does\nannounce or deposit proceed:\n\n**materialize/read-back → durable HEAD success → announce/deposit**\n\nThe engine's published frontier is promoted only after durable HEAD\nsuccess. Replacing `:after-publish` with request and result data must\npreserve that ordering and the existing synchronous local-publication\nbehavior. It must not introduce network waiting or premature success.\nA dissemination failure must not erase an already successful local\ndurable publication: local durability and network acknowledgement are\nseparate outcomes, and the engine reports the round settled on the\nformer alone.\n\nHead following and hydration are distinct. Head following updates the\nexternal resolver; it does not automatically replace the session's local\nindex. Explicit pinned hydration installs recovery before evaluation is\nadmitted. Ordinary following continues without blocking evaluation.\n\n## 4. The dao.jing surface the engine may use\n\nThe current `dao.jing` surface is a content store, not a publication\nservice. There is no existing `dao.jing/submit-publication` or\nrecovery-install function, and HEAD and recovery must not be disguised\nas existing Jing APIs. The engine and its indexing helpers may use the\nfollowing existing entry points, with the contracts as approved in the\nsupplement. These are the only `dao.jing` signatures the engine side of\nthe seam may depend on; a replacement backend must satisfy them all.\n\n| Existing entry point | Contract |\n|---|---|\n| `dao.jing/materialize!` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:365`) `[handle payload]`, `[handle payload opts]` | Canonically encodes once, hashes those bytes, stores them, and returns the segment address after successful insertion or presence verification. `opts` supports `:algorithm`. |\n| `dao.jing/get` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:435`) `[handle address not-found]` | Returns hash-verified, decoded content or the identical supplied sentinel. An invalid address or corrupt content throws. |\n| `dao.jing/observer-state` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:472`) `[members]` | Creates explicit intake state from `{:stream … :cursor …}` members. |\n| `dao.jing/observe-step!` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:523`) `[handle state]` | Materializes at most one payload; returns successor state, stream signal and, on success, address. A throwing write does not advance its cursor. |\n| `dao.jing/adopt-cursor` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:512`) `[state index cursor]` | Explicit cursor adoption only; no implicit recovery or cursor arithmetic. |\n| `dao.jing/segment-key` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:175`) `[v]`, `[v opts]` | Derives a content address independently of backend. |\n| `dao.jing/segment-address?` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:223`), `dao.jing/segment-matches?` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:255`) | Generic address and payload validation. |\n| `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:153`) `[v]` | Canonical encoding for generic sizing and verification where required. |\n| `dao.jing/close!` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457`) `[handle]` | Existing generic lifecycle operation; composition owns its invocation for an injected store. |\n\nThe underlying handle is plain data with these actual signatures:\n\n```clojure\n{:put-bytes-fn (fn [address bytes] ...)       ; => :inserted | :present, or throws\n :get-bytes-fn (fn [address not-found] ...)   ; => bytes | identical not-found\n :close-fn     (fn [] ...)}                  ; optional\n```\n\nEvery replacement backend must provide immutable, address-consistent\nsnapshots; idempotent insertion; immediate read-after-success; unchanged\nnot-found identity; and explicit failure. It cannot overwrite content,\nreturn queued network work as insertion success, or require the engine\nto step transport before a successful local write becomes readable.\nRetained content must remain available for the lifetime of live index\nreferences.\n\nPersistence is a declared property. The memory backend retains content\nfor its lifetime; a persistent backend must satisfy its crash-durability\npromise. Network replication is a separate outcome and is never reported\nas local durability. `materialize!` verifies a `:present` result by\nreading and comparing bytes; it trusts an `:inserted` report, so the\npublication path's explicit read-back remains necessary.\n\nSemantic operations above Jing remain available and unchanged in role.\n`dao.space.transactor/publish!` (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:304`)\n`[log]`, `[log opts]` builds and emits an index publication into intake.\n`dao.space.index/read-manifest` (`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:306`)\n`[content-store manifest-address]` fetches and validates a manifest.\n`dao.space.index/read-datoms` (`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:332`)\n`[content-store manifest-address]` walks EAVT through `jing/get`; it\ndoes not alone validate all four index closures.\n`dao.space.index/restore`\n(`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1344`)\n`[candidate {:keys [content-store intake mode schema ref-type verify?]}]`\nrestores index state; its current signature lacks a byte-policy input,\nwhich is required implementation work under section 5.\n`yin.repl.index/rehydrate` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:189`)\n`[indexer {:keys [manifest datoms]}]` installs recovered history and\nderives allocator authority from it.\n`yin.repl.index/commit-names` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:490`)\n`[indexer signed round]` records supplied envelope and proof facts as\none transaction.\n\nThe new seam, distinct from those existing signatures, is the engine's\ninjected inputs and its publication protocol. Engine construction\nextends `create-state`'s options with the following proposed inputs.\nThese are the seam to implement; they are not existing APIs:\n\n```clojure\n;; Proposed engine construction inputs, extending create-state's options:\n{:index-store        jing-handle\n :publication-policy {:branching-factor n\n                      :max-payload-bytes b} ; nil means no declared byte ceiling\n :publication-out    request-writer\n :publication-in     result-reader\n :publication-cursor minted-result-cursor\n :recovery           {:manifest address-or-nil :datoms datoms-or-nil}\n :retain-index-on-reset? boolean\n ;; Generic link/call media and module registrations supplied separately.\n }\n\n;; Publication request, emitted only after content/read-back succeeds:\n{:op :publish\n :id id\n :round round\n :manifest manifest-address}\n\n;; Matching local publication result:\n{:op :publication-result\n :id id\n :round round\n :manifest manifest-address\n :status :published              ; or :refused, with :reason\n :durability :persistent}        ; :memory for the ephemeral composition\n```\n\nThe records travel on the existing `dao.stream/append!`\n`[handle value]` and `dao.stream/next` `[handle cursor]` operations.\nIdentifiers must be unique within the session and must survive\nevaluation rollback. Stream append success means the request was\naccepted; it does not mean publication completed.\n\nTwo explicit engine operations complete the seam, with these proposed\nsignatures:\n\n```clojure\nyin.repl/accept-publication-result [state result] => state'\nyin.repl/install-recovery [state {:keys [manifest datoms]}] => state'\n```\n\nTheir required behavior is normative. The engine retains the prepared\nfrontier separately and promotes the manifest, published count, and\nverified cursor only for the matching successful result. A refusal\npreserves the prior published frontier, and duplicate or stale results\ncannot promote another round. Synchronous local completion is preserved\nat the composition's public evaluation boundary: the composition drives\nthe engine, services local publication, and delivers the result before\nreporting that round settled. The engine never waits for network\nacknowledgement and never receives a callback. Recovery is installed\nbefore evaluation is admitted. Composition validates the manifest and\nall four reachable indexes, following the existing `validated-snapshot`\nbehavior (`src/cljc/dao/space/store.cljc:192`). Installation derives\nentity and transaction authority from history and discards saved live\ncursors. Publication policy is immutable for a live publication context;\na changed or unknown byte policy requires closure revalidation or\nreconstruction before reuse. The manifest remains exactly\n`{:indexes … :count … :branching-factor …}` throughout.\n\n## 5. Publication policy and frame budgets\n\nR5 lives at the transport/store composition boundary. The effective\ntransport configuration is resolved once and used consistently for the\nDHT state, the store refusal, and the declared payload capability. The\ncurrent wiring constructs the store front using defaults\n(`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:281`); supporting configurable budgets\nmust update that wiring too, or the three consumers of the configuration\nwill disagree.\n\nThe payload budget is derived, never hardcoded. It must satisfy:\n\n```text\nmaximum encoded store-message size(payload-budget) + margin ≤ effective message bound\n```\n\nThe derivation uses the actual wire encoder, the maximal query\nidentifier, the cookie, the supported address forms, and the byte-string\nheader widths. A measured envelope for one payload size is insufficient\nas a universal subtraction. The final full-message refusal is preserved\nas the last resort. Neither `dao.jing` nor the engine acquires DHT\nsemantics under this derivation, and `dao.space` remains responsible for\nsemantic page cuts: the transport composition states how many bytes a\nmessage may carry, and storage decides where pages are cut within that\nbudget.\n\nThe hardcoded publication branching factor `64` in the current\ncomposition (`src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:367–371`), introduced as a\nmessage-size workaround, dies when generic byte-bounded publication\nactivates. It is not relocated as permanent policy. The generic\npublication policy that replaces it is the immutable\n`:publication-policy` of section 4, carrying `:branching-factor` and\n`:max-payload-bytes`, where a `nil` byte ceiling means no declared byte\nceiling.\n\nThe manifest schema does not change. The store composition may supply a\ngeneric `dao.space` publication profile containing `:branching-factor`\nand the byte capability, and the comparison at\n`src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:368` can be restated against that declared\npolicy without a manifest schema change. Strictly, the branching factor\nis an index construction policy, not an intrinsic byte-store capacity.\nThe manifest stays exactly `{:indexes … :count … :branching-factor …}`,\nand its branching factor continues to mean the count cap used for\nconstruction and restoration. That comparison is preserved against the\ngeneric publication policy, retaining the other frontier checks.\nBudget compatibility is tracked in live composition state. A changed or\nunknown policy requires revalidation or reconstruction, not a new\ncontent-hashed manifest field.\n\nThe charter's two related storage corrections are part of this policy.\nFirst, the count-cap compatibility check protects frontier reuse, but\nthe subsequent `restore` currently receives no publication budget\n(`src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:368`). The byte policy must be threaded\nthrough bulk, session, restore, and incremental publication so both\npaths see the same number. Second, the recorder consulted for reuse\ncontains emitted dirty blobs, not necessarily every reachable blob\n(`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1180`). Checking it alone cannot certify\na reused graph against a newly lowered budget. Reuse is permitted only\nunder the same policy; otherwise the closure is verified or rebuilt\nbefore publishing.\n\nByte grouping also changes occupancy assumptions in the B-tree. Large\nseparator datoms can prevent branch grouping from reducing the node\ncount (`src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:2031`). Underfull-node mutation\nbehavior and a terminating refusal for an unrepresentable tree must be\nspecified and tested before byte cuts are enabled. These are storage\nbehaviors, verified in section 8, not composition wiring.\n\n## 6. Coupling inventory — the refactor checklist\n\nPaths below are relative to `src/cljc/`. Locations identify the current\ncode, before relocation. This inventory is the refactor checklist: each\nrow must reach its disposition before Gate A can pass. A literal search\nfinds nine `dao.jing.dht` references inside `yin/repl/`, plus one in\nsibling `yin/repl.cljc`:\n\n| File:line | What it does | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:3` | References the DHT design in module documentation. | **moves to composition** |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:520,522,524,527,528,529` | Renders `solo`, `unpublished`, `too-few-peers`, `oversize`, `absent`, and `busy` reasons. | **moves to composition** |\n| `yin/repl/store.cljc:37` | Describes the DHT-backed store scheme. | **moves to composition** |\n| `yin/repl/store.cljc:126` | Documents the DHT inbound-byte bound. | **moves to composition** |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:877` | Describes engine construction of a DHT node. | **dies** from the engine contract; composition documents its own construction. |\n\nThat literal inventory is insufficient by itself. Most executable\ncoupling uses `dao.space.dht`, `yin.vm.linker.dht`, or `repl.dht`\naliases. The following completes the checklist, including ownership and\nconfiguration dependencies. Target composition namespaces are the\nproposed `yin.shell.*` family outside `yin.repl`.\n\n| File:line | What it does | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:55`, `65–69` | Imports DHT, signed-name linker, head follower/board, and signing services. | **moves to composition**: relocate the composition module to `yin.shell.dht`. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:73–92` | Selects inbound capacity and repair-slot defaults. | **moves to composition**; repair capacity remains measurement-driven. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:94–224` | Parses, validates, reads, and renders `heads.edn`; refuses invalid head startup. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:226–264` | Builds the publisher board and deposits signed head traces; calls `head/deposit!` at `237`, `head/board!` at `253`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:266–335` | Validates followed principals and restores the follower; `head/follow` at `324`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:337–379` | Composes follower/publisher state, starts pinned hydration (`360`), obtains DHT store (`366`), wraps HEAD with announce/deposit (`372–375`), and returns `:dht`. | **moves to composition**. Only the generic content handle and recovery data cross inward. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:367–371` | Hardcodes publication branching factor `64` as a message-size workaround. | **dies** when generic byte-bounded publication activates; do not relocate it as permanent policy. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:389–440` | Opens durable storage, joins transport (`422`), chooses bind implementation (`429`), and cleans up failed assembly (`439`). | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:443–448` | `without-runner` removes the node from a mixed store/node handle. | **dies**. Construct separate engine and composition values initially. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:451–507` | Accesses follower; stops dials/board; checks stopped state; performs final board servicing. Calls at `472,476,487,502–503`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:511–640` | Renders DHT publication/load/head events, acknowledgements, failures, and join tokens; includes `ack-peers` at `525` and event decoding at `577,585,622`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:642–725` | Serves/steps the head board, interprets bind events, and renders board lifecycle. Calls at `651,683,688`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:728–745` | Reads hydrated datoms (`734`), persists local HEAD (`735`), deposits trace, and directly replaces engine index state. | Loading/persistence **moves to composition**; installation **becomes a dao.jing-generic call** carrying validated recovery data. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:748–897` | Reconnects/dials followers, attaches handles, persists accepted heads before installation, retries waiting candidates. Calls at `759,801,811,813,816,863,875,885`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:900–907` | `linked-registry` derives `{module-name manifest-address}` from a VM registry. | **becomes a dao.jing-generic call**: keep the pure registry projection in a neutral module helper; remove the DHT dependency used to reach it. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:910–1027` | Resolves moved names, compares installed heads, steps followers, and filters source-loss/confirmation/install events. Calls/accesses at `914,931–936,993,1003,1013,1018`. | **moves to composition**. Following changes the resolver, not the local session index. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:1031–1117` | Steps transport (`1044`), decodes bind/load/refusal state (`1048,1060,1071–1072`), gates hydration, reports busy state (`1101`), and prints followed heads (`1117`). | **moves to composition**; engine receives generic completions and explicit recovery installation. |\n| `yin/repl/dht.cljc:1120–1156` | Renders DHT startup banner and configuration warnings. | **moves to composition**. |\n\nEngine, index, and query touchpoints:\n\n| File:line | What it does | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `yin/repl.cljc:22` | Requires `yin.repl.dht`. | **dies**. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:249–258,844–897` | DHT CLI/help and construction documentation, including key/follower configuration. | DHT-specific text **moves to composition**; engine documentation describes generic injected services. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:775,792–793` | Constructs the linker; obtains publication options and an `:after-publish` callback from the store. | **becomes a dao.jing-generic call**: injected linker media, generic policy, publication request/result media. The callback bridge dies. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:922,930–965` | Selects store implementation; passes `:dht?`, principals, key and follow configuration; calls `followed-principals`/`open`. This is the charter's `942` region. | **moves to composition**. Engine construction accepts a prepared handle and service endpoints. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:977–992` | Strips runner, retains node, store spec, recovery, and signing key in engine state. | Node/spec/key ownership **moves to composition**; `:dht` and `:dht-key` engine fields **die**. Recovery remains generic. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:1279,1293` | Carries/restores DHT successor through evaluation failure. | **moves to composition**; engine rollback retains generic settlement identities/cursors only. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:1303–1307` | Builds service context including DHT signing key. | Key-bearing context **moves to composition**; requesting round/module/primitive data remains explicit request data. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:1357–1376` | Calls linker with node; calls query service with node; installs both successors. | **moves to composition**. Engine drives/consumes generic link and call streams. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:2069–2075` | Advances linker/node on ticks and compares node successors. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl.cljc:2085–2091` | Filters `:dao.space.dht/event` and `:yin.head/event` against parked manifest/principal waits. | **moves to composition**; engine receives correlated generic readiness/completion events. |\n| `yin/repl/index.cljc:140–166`, `419–450` | Stores/calls `after-publish`, then promotes the published frontier. | **becomes a dao.jing-generic call**: prepare publication, emit request, promote only upon matching publication success. |\n| `yin/repl/index.cljc:368–377,402–412` | Compares manifest count cap with `publish-opts`; restores and publishes reused trees. | **becomes a dao.jing-generic call** using explicit immutable publication policy. Preserve count-cap comparison; add byte-policy compatibility and restore threading. |\n| `yin/repl/index.cljc:490–525` | Commits signed name-envelope data and publishes it, currently including synchronous HEAD callback. | **becomes a dao.jing-generic call**. Composition supplies envelopes; indexer records them without signing, resolving, or announcing. |\n| `yin/repl/index.cljc:13,276,493` | DHT-design references in otherwise generic storage documentation. | DHT references **die**; document the generic materialization/publication guarantees. |\n| `yin/repl/query.cljc:66,73,78–79` | Imports DHT, REPL DHT helpers, signed-name linker, and head services. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/query.cljc:53–61,93–142,177–230,235–248` | Defines and automatically activates `dao.space.dht`, `yin.link`, and `yin.head` host modules. | **moves to composition**. Inject module registrations through a generic mechanism; retain ordinary `dao.space.query` locally. |\n| `yin/repl/query.cljc:648–745` | DHT status/refusal conversion and load/status/retry/cancel/query/module operations. Calls at `654,708–731`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/query.cljc:748–866` | Dispatches head/link operations, derives linked modules (`764`), resolves names (`792`), publishes signed names (`809`), commits envelopes (`818`), reads heads/moves (`860–863`). | DHT services **move to composition**; neutral registry projection and envelope commit use generic calls. |\n| `yin/repl/query.cljc:874–925` | Owns a DHT node in `serve`, dispatches remote services, returns node/indexer successors. | Remote dispatch **moves to composition**. Local query serving retains bounded request/response semantics without node state. |\n\nCLI, startup, and lifecycle touchpoints:\n\n| File:line | What it does | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `yin/repl/store.cljc:9,28,35–181` | DHT scheme parsing, datagram validation, defaults, Jolt refusal, and checked specs. | **moves to composition**, into the store-selection factory. |\n| `yin/repl/store.cljc:184–199` | Opens memory/file stores and redirects DHT opening. | Factory **moves to composition**; the DHT redirect **dies**. |\n| `yin/repl/store.cljc:202–216`; `yin/repl.cljc:1846,1904` | Uses concrete durable-directory state to choose reset behavior and close resources. | **becomes a dao.jing-generic call** for reset policy; resource ownership **moves to composition**. Supply reset-retention policy explicitly rather than deriving it from `:durable-dir`. |\n| `yin/repl/state.cljc:1–27,44–69` | Saves DHT flags, distinguishes one-shot flags, parses trust principals. | Saved configuration **moves to composition**. Neutral principal parsing may move to the existing signing/admission layer; engine trust commands must not import CLI state. |\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc:28` | Requires REPL DHT composition. | **moves to composition** with the host entrypoint. |\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc:78–350,375–497,526–631` | Peer/follow/principal/key/token parsing, DHT subcommands, store specs and flags. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc:634–656,821–889` | Constructs engine with DHT options, loads key, injects WebSocket/head persistence, prints followed heads (`886`). | **moves to composition**; construction hands only generic inputs inward. |\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc:720–780,942–989` | DHT help and banners; `banner` call at `969`. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc:933–934` | Closes head services and store. | **moves to composition** under one resource owner. |\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc:1074,1077,1080,1115,1134` | Steps DHT, handles refusal/admission, keeps ticker busy, chooses exit status. | **moves to composition**. |\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc:1195,1205,1218,1232,1274,1521,1659` | Steps/stops/drains DHT services across shared, Node, and Dart shutdown paths. | **moves to composition**. |\n\nThe search found no additional executable DHT references in the other\nfiles under `src/cljc/yin/repl/`. The linker follow-through of section 2\napplies in addition to this table: the engine's dependency closure must\nalso stop loading the DHT implementation through\n`yin.vm.linker.interpreter`.\n\n## 7. Adapter dispositions\n\nThe four adapters that currently carry DHT knowledge inside the engine\nare replaced by explicit stream interpreters on the composition side.\nNone of them is an injected callback, and none is an opaque runner whose\ninternal state the engine continues to manipulate. Each consumes\ncorrelated generic media and returns progress the engine can observe\nwithout naming a node.\n\n| Adapter | Target composition-side interface |\n|---|---|\n| **Linker progression** | Proposed `serve-links [composition budget] => {:composition … :progress? …}` consumes the existing link request/response media. Composition owns the current `link/round [linker node]` successor; the engine sees correlated decisions and generic pending-work identities, never the node. |\n| **Query services** | Proposed `serve-services [composition budget] => {:composition … :progress? …}` interprets externally registered `dao.space.dht`, `yin.link`, and `yin.head` calls through `dao.stream.apply` envelopes. Local `dao.space.query` stays engine-side; signed publication requests a generic envelope commit and awaits local publication completion. |\n| **Rollback state** | Service cursors, settled request identities, linker successor, and transport state remain in composition across VM rollback. Generic retirement/abandon records identify the run; engine rollback preserves its minted identities and consumes existing settlements without replaying side effects. |\n| **Event filtering** | Proposed `completion-events [composition events] => [generic-event …]` translates load/head outcomes to correlated request or dependency identities. Engine rechecks only matching parked work, ignores unrelated events, and resumes each completion once; DHT/head keywords never cross inward. |\n\nThe engine's side of each adapter is data only: request records,\ncompletion records, and the generic operations of section 4. Composition\nowns the node, interprets the events, and renders any DHT-specific\noutcome before a generic result crosses inward.\n\n## 8. Invariants to pin before relocation\n\nA partially red integration suite permits starting the refactor only\nwhen failures are classified and narrower invariant tests provide a\ntrustworthy oracle. The following invariants are pinned green, at the\nnarrower level, before their implementation moves. Known failing\nend-to-end fixtures remain as failing acceptance tests until the\ncompleted refactor fixes them. If a Node compilation or composition\nfailure prevents those pins from running, that test path is restored\nfirst: absence of results is not evidence of preservation.\n\n**Frontier gate.** Manifest identity, published transaction count,\ncount-cap compatibility, cursor ownership, and expected suffix length\nmust agree. Stale, ahead, or foreign cursors reconstruct fully. Missing\ncontent or failed HEAD persistence must not advance the frontier.\nExisting fixtures include `test/yin/repl/index_test.cljc:385` and\n`test/yin/repl/index_test.cljc:421`.\n\n**Restore Settings threading.** The manifest's count cap reaches every\nrestored node and recording storage. Strong session references and\ndurable refault sources remain intact. Recovery derives allocator\nauthority from history rather than saved process cursors. Byte policy\nreaches the restored publisher separately.\n\n**Repair cadence.** One owner advances transport state using supplied\ntime. Fresh writes retain capacity while repairs progress; queues stay\nbounded; delayed future repairs do not imply perpetual busy status. The\ntwenty-publication/400-tick fixture's requirement for both active repair\nand subsequent idle periods is preserved.\n\n**Cross-host parity.** Identical supported inputs, publication path, and\npolicy yield identical bytes and addresses across JVM, Node, and Dart.\nBulk and incremental paths need logical equivalence; they need not\nproduce identical tree shapes.\n\n**Lifecycle and causality.** Hydration gates input; failed bind or load\nrefuses rather than starting empty; `heads.edn` persistence precedes head\ninstallation; relevant completions resume parked work once; rollback\npreserves already-settled effects; shutdown closes resources once.\n\nStorage acceptance, once the boundary is established, additionally\nrequires bulk and incremental publication, restore and mutation, empty\nand singleton trees, oversized input refusal, manifest compatibility,\nand cross-host determinism. Integration acceptance requires the affected\nJVM, Node, and Dart matrix, with slow test bodies actually enabled. The\nrepository's scoped gate ladder is followed; repository-wide green is\nnot a prerequisite for starting this work.\n\n## 9. Commit sequence and exit conditions\n\nThe refactor proceeds as a reviewable sequence on its own branch.\nIntermediate commits may remain on the refactor branch. The completed\nboundary change merges only when the affected DHT-family tests pass,\nincluding acknowledgement, the Dart ticker-idle fixture, and the\nidentified Node composition failure. DHT-family reds are not greened\nfirst on the coupled architecture, and neither fix 5's `64` override nor\n`cb2cbe11` is merged as a prerequisite baseline repair. Their\nmeasurements remain useful evidence; their placement and constants are\nnot architectural commitments.\n\nBoundary-neutral reds — the PHP frontend installation, the compile-EOF\nregression pin, the golden correction, the stack-size prepend, and Linux\nV8 sizing — may merge before or during this work as isolated changes,\neach gated on its previously failing tests and affected host behavior.\nThe prepend must preserve arguments, namespace enumeration, output, and\nexit status. Stack sizing must produce the guest recursion error rather\nthan a host crash.\n\nR1–R3 implementation may proceed against the agreed generic policy while\nextraction proceeds, but their DHT activation belongs after the boundary\nis established. They are not a prerequisite patch to the existing\nREPL/DHT seam. Their independently reviewed commits are preferably merged\nwith the completed refactor stack.\n\n| Commit/review step | Exit condition |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Pin baseline contracts and parameterize non-DHT fixtures | Existing invariants green; new publication-result expectations explicit. |\n| 2. Separate engine/composition and extract the four adapters | Engine owns no DHT state or dependency; local synchronous behavior preserved. |\n| 3. Generic byte-budget enforcement across bulk, incremental, restore and reuse | Oversize refusal, terminating branch construction, policy compatibility and unchanged manifest proved. |\n| 4. Wire durable/DHT compositions, CLI, recovery and shutdown | HEAD ordering, hydration admission and resource ownership preserved on every host. |\n| 5. Measure and tune repair scheduling | Fresh writes progress; queues bounded; acknowledgement and ticker-idle fixtures pass. |\n| 6. Independent review, affected host matrix, corrections and landing | Both swap gates and all affected composition/storage acceptance tests pass. |\n\nBefore moving behavior, the contract pins of section 8 are established\nand existing failures are recorded against exact revisions. The merge\ngate is the affected DHT-family tests passing, not an aggregate count:\na known failure closed by signature and no unexplained affected-test\nregression. Section 11 governs how failures are tracked while the work\nis in flight.\n\n## 10. Swappability gates (acceptance criteria)\n\nThe two gates below are the acceptance criteria for the dependency rule\nof section 2. Both must pass for the boundary merge. They are stated as\ninvariants, not as aspirations: a refactor that leaves either gate red\nhas not established the boundary, however clean the namespace layout\nappears.\n\n### Gate A — zero storage-implementation references\n\nRun from the repository root:\n\n```sh\nif rg --hidden --no-ignore -n -F -- 'dao.jing.dht' \\\n    src/cljc/yin/repl/ src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc\nthen\n    exit 1\nelse\n    test \"$?\" -eq 1\nfi\n```\n\nThis includes the requested tree and its sibling engine file, checks\ncomments and docstrings too, and fails on a search error. There is no\nexclusion for a thin shim: DHT composition must leave the tree.\n\nThe same review must reject indirect evasion. Run this companion audit\nand require no implementation dependencies:\n\n```sh\nrg -n \\\n  'dao\\.space\\.dht|yin\\.repl\\.dht|yin\\.vm\\.linker\\.(dht|head)|:dht\\b|--dht-' \\\n  src/cljc/yin/repl/ src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc\n```\n\nThe engine's dependency closure must also stop loading the DHT\nimplementation through `yin.vm.linker.interpreter`. Moving imports into\nanother engine-owned helper does not pass. The audit is over the closure\nthe engine actually loads, not over the files that happen to mention a\nstring.\n\n### Gate B — the engine suite passes with explicitly injected non-DHT storage\n\nUse a common test constructor and composition fixture, with two backend\nselections:\n\n- Memory: the existing `dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem []`.\n- File: the existing `dao.space.store/open [dir]`, which wraps\n  `dao.jing.file/create-content-file [path]` and supplies locking, HEAD,\n  and validated recovery.\n\nThe current injection point already exists:\n\n```clojure\n(repl.frontends/create-state {:index-store handle ...})\n```\n\nFor the refactor, the fixture passes the pure byte handle plus the new\npolicy, recovery, and media inputs of section 4 separately. It owns the\npublication interpreter: the memory composition acknowledges local\ncompletion without claiming persistence; the file composition calls the\nexisting `:head-fn [manifest-address]`, then emits success or refusal.\nIt pumps local requests and results and closes resources. It constructs\nno DHT node, socket, follower, or repair scheduler.\n\nThe retained engine suite is parameterized over this fixture; the proof\ndoes not merely rely on default memory construction. Linker tests\nreceive the explicit local `:content-store`/`:name-env` arrangement\nalready used at `test/yin/repl/require_test.cljc:134`.\n\nThe decisive subset is:\n\n| Proof | Existing fixtures to retain/adapt |\n|---|---|\n| Evaluation and all supported VM paths | `yin.repl-test`; `yin.repl.index-test/every-evaluator-is-indexed-the-same-way`; AST index/query tests. |\n| Publication through the injected handle | `index_test.cljc:146,269,316,344,362,522,576,592`. |\n| Frontier compatibility and failure | `index_test.cljc:385,421,553,734`: stale/foreign cursors, unreadable dirty content, HEAD failure, and read-back ordering. |\n| Recovery and continued allocation | `index_test.cljc:470,501`; file fixtures at `store_test.cljc:1009,1217,1372`. |\n| Corruption/refusal, reset and lifecycle | `store_test.cljc:833,851,874,976,1056–1121,1298,1425`. Preserve intended memory/file reset differences. |\n| Local linking and parked-work causality | `require_test.cljc:244,263,346,437,696,740,766`. Move its explicitly DHT-specific construction test at `805` to composition tests. |\n| Query settlement survives rollback | `query_test.cljc:283,314,663`, including the guarded slow body. |\n\nNarrow tests are added for the genuinely new protocol: delayed or failed\npublication results do not promote the frontier; duplicate or wrong-round\nresults cannot settle it; rollback does not replay a settled service\nrequest. A fresh engine-only JVM test process is started and the test\nasserts that the DHT namespaces were not loaded.\n\nThe whole retained engine suite, including this subset, is required on\nJVM, Node, and Dart, with guarded bodies enabled (`DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`\nwhere applicable). Actual executed tests are recorded, not only\nnamespace loading.\n\nDHT-specific tests move with composition. They are not deleted and are\nnot counted as the non-DHT proof. The boundary merge still requires their\nexisting assertions — including eight distinct acknowledged publications,\nthe twenty-round/400-tick idle fixture, head-follow persistence,\nbind/hydration refusal, and shutdown — to pass on the affected host\nmatrix. One correction from the charter applies to that set: the \"eight\ndistinct\" assertion at `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:2097` counts manifest\naddresses, not acknowledgement timestamps. A failure there is diagnosed\nas failed publication or HEAD advancement, not relaxed as a supposed\ntiming pin.\n\n## 11. Nightly exposure during the refactor window\n\nAn exact failure ledger is maintained, keyed by revision, host and\nruntime, test identifier, assertion signature, reproduction command,\nevidence path, and responsible change. Master failures are distinguished\nfrom workaround-branch regressions: the recorded Dart evidence says\nticker-idle passed on master `3d2f20db` and failed with fix 5.\n\nAffected lanes continue to execute and complete logs are retained. A\nresult is classified as a known boundary failure only when its signature\nmatches the recorded failure. A new assertion, timeout, compile failure,\nmissing namespace, skipped slow body, or incomplete runner summary is a\nnew or unverified result, even inside an already-red namespace.\n\nNew failures are reproduced narrowly, then base and candidate are\ncompared under the same environment. The responsible change is held\nuntil resolved. Exclusions are not widened, pins are not weakened, and an\nunchanged aggregate failure count is not treated as \"no regression.\"\nUnrelated verified fixes may continue merging. The boundary merge\nrequires its known failures closed and no unexplained affected-test\nregressions.\n\nTwo charter corrections to common misreadings belong here. The earlier\nproposal that promoted transport chunks and acknowledgements into the\nstream contract is superseded by the owner ruling of section 1:\nfragmentation, reassembly, and transport acknowledgements stay below the\nlogical stream interface. The existing generic `dao.stream` API\nadditionally describes whole values; this refactor does not authorize\nrewriting those values or its operations into a universal chunk\nprotocol.\n\n## 12. R-set adaptation\n\n| Requirement | Disposition |\n|---|---|\n| **R1** | **Core survives in storage; not wholly as written.** Encode-once length accounting is sound for legal canonical leaf/branch encodings. Include separator datoms, addresses, framing, and level encoding. An oversized singleton may exist temporarily during planning, but must not escape as a successful publication. Branch construction must reduce or explicitly refuse. |\n| **R2** | **Survives with completion requirements.** Check before intake emission; rebuild an oversized incremental index through the bounded bulk path. Use clean staging so rejected blobs and cached addresses cannot leak into the replacement publication. Validate the manifest too. Handle reused clean subgraphs under changed budgets. Recorder length checks need no extra encodes; rebuilding does. |\n| **R3** | **Schema/count-cap rule survives as written.** Preserve configurable count caps, including existing small-factor fixtures; 512 remains the default, not a universal requirement. Retire the DHT-specific 64 override when bounded publication is active. Underfull restore/mutation behavior remains a required proof. |\n| **R4** | **Measurement principle survives; sequencing is replaced.** Neither blanket preservation nor blanket reversal of `cb2cbe11` is justified. Measure repair capacity in the new composition before merge. No entitlement attaches to 16, 40, or 48 slots. |\n| **R5** | **Moves into composition design.** Transport derives the capability; storage consumers receive generic policy. Full-message verification survives. Hardcoded REPL/index transport budgets die. |\n| **R6** | **Survives and broadens.** Measure real REPL leaf and branch sizes, full wire sizes, counts per index, distinct addresses per round, repair occupancy, and idle behavior before pinning configuration. Include bulk, suffix, and restart paths. |\n| **R7** | **Explicitly overturned.** Byte-cutting the existing coupled REPL composition on `nightly-reds-fix` before extraction is no longer the approved sequence. Worktree reconciliation, independent review, and cross-host verification remain valid requirements. |\n\n## 13. Related debt: native recursion over deep structures\n\nThis section records a debt family the refactor must not worsen. It is\nnot a merge gate for the boundary, but any helper the refactor touches\nis held to it.\n\nHost helpers must not recurse over deep structures. A traversal whose\ndepth is a function of stored content — an index tree, a canonical\nencoding, a recovered datom closure — will meet content deeper than any\nhost stack the three targets guarantee. Native recursion over such a\nstructure turns valid stored content into a host crash, and the crash\narrives only at the depth production data reaches, not at the depth\ntests reach. The portable form is an explicit worklist or an iterative\nwalk with heap-allocated state, so depth costs heap, which is accounted,\nrather than native stack, which is not.\n\nThree instances of the family are in scope for the code this refactor\ntouches, and each is audited when its file is next edited for the\nboundary:\n\n- canonical encoding and sizing on the publication path\n  (`dao.jing/canonical-bytes` and the encode-once accounting of\n  section 5), where a deeply nested payload must not overflow the host\n  stack while its byte length is being derived;\n- B-tree blob grouping, branch construction, and restore walks\n  (`dao.data.btree` and `dao.space.index/restore`), where separator\n  datoms and tree depth grow with the stored index;\n- recovery validation and index traversal\n  (`dao.space.index/read-manifest`, `read-datoms`, and the\n  `validated-snapshot` closure check), where all four index closures are\n  walked before evaluation is admitted.\n\nThe audit criterion is uniform: for each of the three, a synthetic\nstructure deeper than the host's comfortable native depth must either\ncomplete iteratively or refuse explicitly with a named reason. A\n`StackOverflowError`, a JavaScript range error, or a Dart stack overflow\non valid content is a defect in the helper, not a limit of the content.\nFixing an instance is ordinary iterative rewriting of that helper; it\ndoes not change any contract in sections 2–5 and is scheduled as\nfollow-up debt where the boundary work does not already rewrite the\nhelper.\n\n## 14. Estimate\n\nPlan eight working days; plausible range six to twelve, moderate\nconfidence. The estimate follows the charter's sequence, including its\nstorage and repair work — not just namespace relocation.\n\n| Commit/review step | Working days | Exit condition |\n|---|---:|---|\n| 1. Pin baseline contracts and parameterize non-DHT fixtures | 0.5–1 | Existing invariants green; new publication-result expectations explicit. |\n| 2. Separate engine/composition and extract the four adapters | 1.5–2.5 | Engine owns no DHT state or dependency; local synchronous behavior preserved. |\n| 3. Generic byte-budget enforcement across bulk, incremental, restore and reuse | 1.5–3 | Oversize refusal, terminating branch construction, policy compatibility and unchanged manifest proved. |\n| 4. Wire durable/DHT compositions, CLI, recovery and shutdown | 1–1.5 | HEAD ordering, hydration admission and resource ownership preserved on every host. |\n| 5. Measure and tune repair scheduling | 0.5–1.5 | Fresh writes progress; queues bounded; acknowledgement and ticker-idle fixtures pass. |\n| 6. Independent review, affected host matrix, corrections and landing | 1–2 | Both swap gates and all affected composition/storage acceptance tests pass. |\n| **Total** | **6–11.5** | **Budget eight; reserve twelve.** |\n\nThe estimate assumes 30–90-minute implementation rounds, 1–2-hour\narchitectural review rounds, existing invariant fixtures being reused,\nand the relayed agy/codex/muse/qwen fleet remaining available. It\nincludes review and rework rather than treating the first generated\npatch as finished. It does not assume unlimited parallel speedup: the\nadapter and publication boundary is a shared critical path.\n\nStep 3 is most likely to exceed the estimate. Byte-bounded trees must\nhandle separator size, underfull mutation, non-reducing branch groups,\nand reused clean subgraphs under changed policy. That is substantive\nstorage behavior with cross-host consequences. The runner and adapter\nextraction is the next-largest risk, particularly preserving synchronous\nquery and publication behavior without callbacks or rolled-back service\neffects.\n\n\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
